[7, 4, 1]
Complexity

The target search plus the descendant searches collectively visit O(n) nodes.

Time: O(n)

Auxiliary space: O(h) for recursion, excluding output.

This is an excellent solution when you want to practice recursion and backtracking without an explicit parent map.

Approach 3 — Parent Map + BFS

This is the approach in your supplied code. It is also one of the easiest to understand and implement in interviews.

Idea

A normal binary tree only stores child references:

node → left child
node → right child

To move upward from the target, we first build:

child → parent

Then perform BFS starting at the target.

For each node, explore:

left child
right child
parent

Use a visited set to avoid returning to a node already reached.

The BFS layers represent distance from the target:

Distance 0 → target
Distance 1 → direct neighbors
Distance 2 → nodes two edges away
...
Your supplied code
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int x) { val = x; }
 * }
 */
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> distanceK(TreeNode root, TreeNode target, int k) {
        List<Integer> al=new ArrayList<>();
        Map<Integer, TreeNode> parent=new HashMap<>();
        Queue<TreeNode> q=new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);
        while(!q.isEmpty()){
            int size=q.size();
            for(int i=0;i<size;i++){
                TreeNode curr=q.poll();
                if(curr.left!=null){
                    parent.put(curr.left.val,curr);
                    q.offer(curr.left);
                }
                if(curr.right!=null){
                    parent.put(curr.right.val,curr);
                    q.offer(curr.right);
                }
            }
        }

        Map<Integer, Integer> visited=new HashMap<>();
        q.offer(target);
        while(k>0&&!q.isEmpty()){
            int size=q.size();
            for(int i=0;i<size;i++){
                TreeNode curr=q.poll();
                visited.put(curr.val,1);
                if(curr.left!=null&&!visited.containsKey(curr.left.val)){
                    q.offer(curr.left);
                }
                if(curr.right!=null&&!visited.containsKey(curr.right.val)){
                    q.offer(curr.right);
                }
                if(parent.containsKey(curr.val)&&!visited.containsKey(parent.get(curr.val).val)){
                    q.offer(parent.get(curr.val));
                }
            }
            k--;
        }
            while(!q.isEmpty()){
                al.add(q.poll().val);
        }
        return al;
    }
}

Your algorithm's overall idea is correct for LeetCode 863, where node values are unique.

Two improvements make the code more robust:

Use TreeNode objects as map/set keys rather than node values.

Mark nodes as visited when they are enqueued, rather than when they're removed from the queue.

Also add a null-root guard.

Recommended BFS code
import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public List<Integer> distanceK(
            TreeNode root,
            TreeNode target,
            int k) {

        List<Integer> answer = new ArrayList<>();

        if (root == null || target == null) {
            return answer;
        }

        // Map each node to its parent.
        Map<TreeNode, TreeNode> parent = new HashMap<>();

        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);
        parent.put(root, null);

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {

            TreeNode curr = q.poll();

            if (curr.left != null) {
                parent.put(curr.left, curr);
                q.offer(curr.left);
            }

            if (curr.right != null) {
                parent.put(curr.right, curr);
                q.offer(curr.right);
            }
        }

        // BFS outward from the target.
        Set<TreeNode> visited = new HashSet<>();
        q.offer(target);
        visited.add(target);

        int distance = 0;

        while (!q.isEmpty() && distance < k) {

            int size = q.size();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {

                TreeNode curr = q.poll();

                // Visit left child.
                if (curr.left != null &&
                        visited.add(curr.left)) {
                    q.offer(curr.left);
                }

                // Visit right child.
                if (curr.right != null &&
                        visited.add(curr.right)) {
                    q.offer(curr.right);
                }

                // Visit parent.
                TreeNode par = parent.get(curr);

                if (par != null && visited.add(par)) {
                    q.offer(par);
                }
            }

            distance++;
        }

        // Queue now contains nodes exactly k edges away.
        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            answer.add(q.poll().val);
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
