Why size = q.size() matters

In your code:

int size = q.size();

for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
    ...
}
k--;

This ensures that each iteration processes exactly one distance layer.

For example:

Distance 0: [5]
Distance 1: [6, 2, 3]
Distance 2: [7, 4, 1]

If you didn't capture size at the start, newly discovered nodes could be processed in the same iteration, mixing distances.

