Which approach should you remember?

For the cleanest recursive solution: use DFS + ancestor backtracking.

For the most intuitive BFS solution: use a parent map and BFS from the target. This is your approach and is particularly easy to dry-run in an interview.

Interview pattern to remember
All nodes distance K
        ↓
Need to move up and down
        ↓
Parent pointers OR recursive ancestor search
        ↓
BFS by distance OR DFS + backtracking

The key lesson is that distance-K problems on trees become much easier when you can move in all directions. A parent map turns the tree into an undirected graph, after which ordinary BFS solves the problem in O(n) time.
